# SYNOPSIS

```usage
basename path [suffix]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`basename` prints <path> with everything up to its last `/` removed, then a newline.
Trailing `/`s are ignored first, so `/a/b/` gives `b`; a <path> of `/` alone gives `/`, and an empty <path> an empty line.
If <suffix> is given, not empty, and ends what is left without being all of it, it is removed too: `basename /a/b.c .c` prints `b`, and `basename .c .c` prints `.c`.

`basename` is sbase's, a hard link to `/boot/bin/posix/sbase-box`; there is no native one.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it as `/bin/basename`.

# EXAMPLES

From the sbase test, `tests/posix/sbasetest.c`, which runs each command as a shell would:

```text
command: basename /a/b/c
output:  c
```

# SEE ALSO

dirname(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/basename.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

Fewer than one argument or more than two, or any flag, prints `usage: basename path [suffix]` and exits 1.
It exits 1 if writing the result fails, and 0 otherwise.

# BUGS

An argument beginning with `-` is taken as a flag and refused, unless `--` comes before it.
